ZERO / EZERO 0.10.0 — NATIVE WINDOWS X64 TOOLKIT

The native zero.exe and ezero.exe commands compile with our original Zero
compiler and Ezero frontend/runtime, loaded directly from lib/. They do not
launch CMD, PowerShell, Bash, Python, a subprocess compiler, or any foreign
language runtime. All application, compiler, setup, updater and SHA256 code
is original Zero. Windows operating-system APIs provide host file and memory
services. Keep the complete directory together when moving the toolkit.

Target: Windows 10/11 x64. Execution verification uses Wine10 on Debian13;
a native Microsoft Windows machine run is not claimed. The archive includes
no Wine, Linux runtime, foreign DLL, boot firmware or host test machinery.

COMPILE AND RUN

From a terminal in the extracted directory:

    .\zero.exe examples\hello.zero hello.exe
    .\hello.exe

    .\ezero.exe examples\banana.ezero banana.exe
    .\banana.exe

    .\ezero.exe examples\host-files.ezero copy-text.exe
    .\copy-text.exe input.txt new-output.txt

Ezero source and host text are UTF-8. The host-files example reads and copies
text, preserves existing destination files, and uses one-based arguments.
Use a terminal with UTF-8 display support to show non-ASCII output correctly.
The binary files and UTF-8 path operations do not depend on console encoding.
Quote command-line paths containing spaces using your terminal's normal rules.

    .\zero.exe --help
    .\ezero.exe INPUT.ezero NEW_OUTPUT.exe [TARGET]
    .\zero.exe INPUT.zero NEW_OUTPUT.exe [TARGET]

The default output is Windows PE64. Explicit targets: --host, --windows,
--raw, --raw32, --efi, --efi32, --real, --app64 and --app32. Other targets need
the matching execution environment. Existing output files are never replaced.
Windows accepts63 user arguments and a complete UTF-8 command line of at
most4095 bytes. Invalid arguments and source errors return a failure status.
Companion files are resolved relative to the executable, not the working
folder. A renamed command may select its language explicitly as its first
argument, for example rebuilt.exe zero INPUT.zero NEW_OUTPUT.exe.

OWN SOURCE / REBUILD

source/ contains the complete original standalone implementations. For example:

    .\zero.exe source\compiler-cli.zero rebuilt.exe
    .\rebuilt.exe --help
    .\zero.exe source\compiler.zero rebuilt-compiler.exe
    .\zero.exe source\installer\setup.zero rebuilt-setup.exe
    .\zero.exe source\updater\update.zero rebuilt-update.exe
    .\zero.exe source\updater\sha256.zero rebuilt-sha256.exe

source\compiler-seed.exe is the original direct stream compiler reference;
normal users should use zero.exe/ezero.exe. The native CLI can rebuild this
seed from source\compiler.zero without invoking it as an external process.
The compiler apps in lib/ use our own ZEROAPP1 ABI; they are also reproducible
with --app64 from the included compiler, frontend and packer sources.
The language, memory and ABI documentation is in source\ezero\.

PREPARE ZERO OS FILES

The OS ISO is distributed separately. Use a trusted release ISO:

    .\zero-setup.exe prepare zero.iso NEW_DIRECTORY
    .\zero-setup.exe status NEW_DIRECTORY
    .\zero-setup.exe remove NEW_DIRECTORY

Preparation copies/verifies the ISO and creates original ZeroFS storage plus
an ownership manifest in a new directory. Removal validates ownership and
payloads and preserves unrelated files. Boot integration is NOT IMPLEMENTED:
this does not add a boot-menu entry, install Zero OS into Windows, or make an
ISO inside an arbitrary host filesystem bootable. Boot the separate ISO in a
compatible VM. See source\installer\README.ztext for the exact contract.

LOCAL UPDATE PREPARATION

    .\zero-update.exe init zero.iso 1 NEW_STORE
    .\zero-update.exe stage next-zero.iso 2 NEW_STORE
    .\zero-update.exe status NEW_STORE
    .\zero-update.exe rollback NEW_STORE

    .\zero-update.exe publish next-zero.iso 3 NEW_INBOX
    .\zero-update.exe check NEW_INBOX NEW_STORE
    .\zero-update.exe verify NEW_STORE

These commands prepare and verify local release copies and record a rollback
selection. They do not activate the selected OS, register a startup task or
fetch releases from the Internet. Counters start at1 and must increase for
new staging, including after rollback. Repeated check is idempotent.
SHA256 detects corruption; it does not authenticate a release publisher.
Use trusted local ISO/inbox files. No partition table, boot sector, firmware
entry or user data disk is changed. Interrupted journal writes are rejected;
automatic crash repair and power-loss durability are not claimed. Full state
and recovery rules are in source\updater\README.ztext and FORMAT.ztext.

VERIFY FILES

    .\zero-sha256.exe zero.exe
    .\zero-sha256.exe zero.iso

SHA256SUMS.ztext lists package file hashes. BUILD-INPUTS.sha256 identifies the
original source and compiler inputs used to build the native tools. The
SHA256 implementation is original Zero and has no external hash dependency.

Native Windows reparse handling uses Windows file attributes and open flags.
Wine's Z: POSIX symlinks do not expose those attributes, and Wine10's symbolic
link creation API is a stub. Wine tests therefore do not establish native
Windows reparse-point behavior. The file API does not sandbox linked ancestor
directories; use private folders you own. These tools remain bounded early
system tools, with the detailed capabilities and limits documented alongside
their sources.
